to form a chain of  amino acids which form the protein  so a mistake would be incorporating the  incorrect amino acid into the protein  chain but that's only one amino acid in  a protein chain does that have any  influence  well take the protein p43 for example  the world's coolest protein  you thought i'd forgotten if a mistake  happens in the production of p53 it  could end up doing the wrong thing  because the changes in the amino acid  sequence may influence how the protein  folds or it might affect the activity of  the protein and may not be able to  perform its function in the same way  but you know one mistake on one protein  out of hundreds of that same protein  within a cell it's probably gonna be  okay but what if mistakes keep happening  what if the accuracy of protein  synthesis declines over time could this  be an explanation for the aging process  well it could well be  loss of accuracy and translation comes  under the umbrella of the hallmark of  aging term loss of proteostasis  which broadly refers to the loss of the  ability to maintain correctly folded and  functional proteins in a cell over time  and so far most of the evidence for  supporting a connection between  translational fidelity and aging has  come from single-celled organisms like  yeast  whereby mutations in the ribosome  complex that carries out translation  protein synthesis whereby they become  more error-prone it shortens the  chronological lifespan of the yeast and  so given that proteins are kind of  ubiquitously synthesized within a cell  the ribosome has a bit of a mammoth  challenge on its hands to be able to  accurately synthesize all these proteins  and the ribosome itself is a complex  machinery composed of both protein and  rna and the process of protein synthesis  is a highly energetic one and it's  thought that this energetic process is  coupled to rearrangements within the  ribosome complex to actually try and  enhance the fidelity of protein  synthesis  and that's all due to interactions  between the codons from the mrna  sequence with trna transfer rnas that  carry in each individual amino acids  and so mutations within the ribosome  itself whether it's on the rna  components or the protein components may  alter the structure and dynamics of the  ribosome and therefore influence how the  sequence is read and therefore could  influence the accuracy of translation  so that was probably a bit tmi i mean i  had like three lectures on this as  undergrad so i didn't expect you to  fully understand  but let's take a look at some of the  data from this paper as i think it will  help to make a bit more sense  so what they did first in the study was  perform an unbiased phylogenetic  analysis so basically looking at the  sequences of different ribosome  components across different organisms to  see which ones are conserved in  evolution  as if it's conserved there's likely a  reason for that that is the residue is  important  and so what you're looking at now is  part of the structure of the ribosome  whereby you can see the mrna sequence  that's being read by the ribosome and a  trna one of these transfer rnas that is  going to be bringing in one of the amino  acids to add to the protein gene and  what's indicated in this structure is a  protein component of the ribosome known  as rps23  and the reason we're looking at rps23  is because if you compare the sequences  of this protein from archaea  all the way through to eukaryotes like  us  consistently they found in their  analysis a lysine residue so lysine is  an amino acid consult in a specific  region of this protein so it was nearly  invariant throughout evolution  now i emphasized the word nearly because  there were some exceptions to this rule  where instead of having a lysine residue  they had an arginine residue  and the exceptions were hypothermophilic  archaea and what that means is that  these archaea predominantly are living  in extreme conditions such as high  temperature and acidic environments and  that metabolized sulfur so not somewhere  you'd want to be  anyway the authors of the study thought  this was very interesting and decided to  see what happens if you change this  lysine residue to an arginine and the  implications for protein synthesis  so using a very clever reporter assay  they did this in flies whereby they'd  given these flies to mutation so that  they now had arginine in this rps23  protein instead of lysine and what they  found was that the accuracy of protein  synthesis improved and this improvement  was even seen in older flies as well  where normally you would see a much  greater increase in the amount of errors  made in translation  now this phrase is a very interesting  question if this mutation was improving  the accuracy of protein synthesis  why didn't evolution select for this  mutation  well one hypothesis is the fact that  accuracy is kind of in a trade-off with  speed whereby the more accurate the  process is the slower it takes to  actually produce a protein and so if  you're producing a lot of proteins that  could have downtown consequences of a  reduced growth in the organism which  makes it less competitive  whilst if you want to improve the rate  of protein synthesis it might lose  accuracy as a cost but what the authors  found was that it didn't really seem to  impair the global translation  instead they found in yeast worms and  flies that the presence of this mutation  delayed their development for example in  one of the species of yeast those that  had the mutation  formed smaller colonies and showed  growth retardation in liquid media  on the flip side the yeast flies and  worms that they tested with this  mutation shows heat stress resistance  and so the authors rationalize that the  developmental data explains the presence  of the arginine residue in organisms  that live only in extreme conditions for  which increased translation fidelity is  a strong selective pressure  so that explains why we might not have  this mutation but would having the  mutation be better in a long run for  lifespan  well as you can see here in the yeast  worms and flies that they tested with  this mutation they showed lifespan  extensions mediated by the single change  from nine percent to 23 percent  and so this suggested that this improved  accuracy in the translation provides a  potential novel anti-aging intervention  but are there non-genetic ways of being  able to improve the accuracy of  translation well if you watch my videos  you've probably heard of the compound  rapamycin that inhibits the protein the  mammalian target of rapamycin mtor  and mtor does many many things in a cell  but one of the things that regulates is  translation itself  whereby increased amateur activity has  been shown to improve the rates of  translation  and so a study came out a couple of  years ago that i made a blog on  in the study they showed that by slowing  down the rate of translation it improved  the fidelity of translation so it  improved the accuracy and so this goes  back to that kind of trade-off that i  was talking about earlier so what the  authors tested next was whether or not  they could see similar effects on the  the accuracy of translation  with rapamycin and similar molecules  and whether there would be a synergistic  improvement on accuracy if you combined  rapamycin with this arginine mutation  and well you can see the data here and  basically as before those organisms that  had the rgb mutation live longer  but adding rypomitin only seemed to show  additional benefit in the drosophila to  the flies  but these additional increases were  modest and so basically what this  suggests is that when translation  accuracy is increased the capacity of  rapamycin to extend lifespan is likely  limited to its remaining  organism-specific anti-aging components  basically referring to the fact that  maybe most of the impacts of rapamycin  is due to its improvements on protein  accuracy